{"step_index":1751,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T00:03:25Z","thinking":"**Examining Roman Domination Proof**\n\nThe goal is to prove an upper bound on the Roman domination number of a graph. The current proof structure attempts to leverage the graph's radius. The proof begins by introducing variables and assumptions, then applying an upper bound using the radius.\n\nA Roman dominating function is introduced, and it appears the proof will proceed by bounding its weight. The \"by_cases\" tactic is then used on the cardinality of a type.\n\n\n**Exploring Alternative Uniqueness Assumptions**\n\nIf there are two vertices where the function has value 2, a contradiction arises by showing the Roman weight is at least 4. This uses the subset of all vertices and the known value of the function on the two vertices. However, I need to check the case when the radius is greater than 3.\n\n\n**Generalizing Roman Domination Proof**\n\nThe case of a single vertex labeled 2 is understood; now multiple such vertices require consideration. Focus shifts to analyzing $V \\setminus B_1(u)$ for a vertex *u* with label 2, recognizing that other labeled vertices and their neighborhoods matter. A union $U$ of the 1-balls for all vertices with label 2 is constructed to analyze $V \\setminus U$. Every vertex outside $U$ can only have a value of 1.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/test_card_bound2.lean\"","EndLine":"100","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"View test_card_bound2.lean\"","toolSummary":"\"View test_card_bound2.lean\""}}]}
{"step_index":1752,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T00:03:31Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T02:03:31+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T02:03:31+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/test_card_bound2.lean`\nTotal Lines: 159\nTotal Bytes: 6293\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: import Mathlib\n2: import LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable\n3: \n4: open SimpleGraph\n5: open GraphCalc\n6: \n7: lemma minUpTo_le {n : ℕ} {p : ℕ → Prop} [DecidablePred p] (k : ℕ) (hk : k ≤ n) (hp : p k) :\n8:     minUpTo n p ≤ k := by\n9:   unfold minUpTo\n10:   have hmem : k ∈ (Finset.range (n + 1)).filter p := by\n11:     rw [Finset.mem_filter, Finset.mem_range]\n12:     exact ⟨Nat.lt_succ_of_le hk, hp⟩\n13:   have hmin := Finset.min_le hmem\n14:   generalize hmin_eq : ((Finset.range (n + 1)).filter p).min = m\n15:   rw [hmin_eq] at hmin\n16:   cases m with\n17:   | top => contradiction\n18:   | coe val =>\n19:     simp only [WithTop.coe_le_coe] at hmin\n20:     exact hmin\n21: \n22: theorem radius_le_eccentricity {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n23:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : V) :\n24:     GraphCalc.radius G ≤ GraphCalc.eccentricity G v := by\n25:   unfold GraphCalc.radius\n26:   have hmem : GraphCalc.eccentricity G v ∈ Finset.image (GraphCalc.eccentricity G) Finset.univ := by\n27:     apply Finset.mem_image_of_mem\n28:     exact Finset.mem_univ v\n29:   obtain ⟨b, hb⟩ := Finset.min_of_mem hmem\n30:   rw [hb]\n31:   have hle : (Finset.image (GraphCalc.eccentricity G) Finset.univ).min ≤ ↑(GraphCalc.eccentricity G v) :=\n32:     Finset.min_le hmem\n33:   rw [hb] at hle\n34:   exact WithTop.coe_le_coe.mp hle\n35: \n36